# Mismatches that are understood.  Anything NOT listed here fails the
# run; this file is the difference between a check and a tool that
# always says no.
#
# Format:  <file>:<name> <tab> reason
#
# KEYED BY FILE, not by name alone.  It was keyed by name, and one entry
# written about os/inferno/port/devdynld.c silently excused the same
# mismatch in emu/port/devdynldx.c -- a second file, in a different
# build, that nobody had looked at.  A baseline that over-matches is the
# same defect as a check that cannot fail.
#
# EMPTY, AND THAT IS THE POINT.  It had two entries and neither
# survived being looked at properly:
#
#   kdynloadfd  was FIXED rather than excused.  devdynld.c and
#               devdynldx.c each declared and called four parameters
#               where every implementation defines three.
#
#   log         was NEVER A MISMATCH.  The entry said devbench.c's
#               log(char*, ...) collides with libc's double log(double)
#               declared in lib.h, and called it unfixable.  It is
#               `static void log(char *msg, ...)' -- file-local, so it
#               cannot collide at link time and lib.h's declaration
#               cannot refer to it.  The entry described a non-problem
#               and was written before this tool knew about static.
#
# So: add nothing here that has not survived the question "could the
# linker actually resolve that declaration to that definition".  Each
# line is a claim someone verified; re-verify before adding one.
